import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"which representation is canonical: the calculus for laws, the op-graph for execution","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"which representation is canonical: the calculus for laws, the op-graph for execution","type":"adr","date":"2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z","status":"proposed"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/adrs/0011-canonical-representation.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/adrs/0011-canonical-representation.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/adrs/0011-canonical-representation.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0011-—-which-representation-is-canonical-the-calculus-for-laws-the-op-graph-for-execution" tabindex="-1">ADR 0011 — which representation is canonical: the calculus for laws, the op-graph for execution <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0011-—-which-representation-is-canonical-the-calculus-for-laws-the-op-graph-for-execution" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR 0011 — which representation is canonical: the calculus for laws, the op-graph for execution”">​</a></h1><p><strong>Depends on</strong>: ADR 0002 (categorical re-expression), ADR 0003 (four dimensions), ADR 0008 (combinator presentation — <em>and</em> its two-way-drift warning), ADR 0009 (the ladder; stage 3 names this decision), <strong>ADR 0010 (one language, many interpretations)</strong>. <strong>Informed by</strong>: spike Q0 (catopt&#39;s vocabulary assembles as handl data — PASS, <code>handl b20b978a</code>, catopt <code>8d5b47d</code>) and spike Q1a (a tensor crosses by reference as a <code>ForeignResource</code>).</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>Two representations of the same programs coexist:</p><ol><li><strong>The op-graph IR</strong> (<code>catopt_core.ir</code>) — what the sinks lower, what the cost model prices, what the certificate replays against.</li><li><strong>The arrow calculus</strong> — <code>lab/bridge/combinator.py</code> today, handl&#39;s <code>Term</code> + <code>Language</code> in the direction ADR 0010 adopts.</li></ol><p>ADR 0008 warned that two <em>peers</em> drift: every law must exist in both, or one becomes second-class. ADR 0009 deferred the question (&quot;decide the canonical representation — its own ADR&quot;) rather than answering it. The spike has now measured the facts the decision needs:</p><ul><li><strong>Q0 (PASS)</strong> — catopt&#39;s vocabulary <em>is</em> handl data: a <code>Language</code> assembles from catopt-emitted fragments; a law lands in <code>lang.transforms</code> with its fields intact; the <code>catopt:</code> extension field is ignored by handl&#39;s serde and therefore rides along <strong>losslessly</strong>. Two representational requirements were found and fixed (fold <code>when</code> into <code>And([...])</code>; spell enum variants as YAML tags).</li><li><strong>Q1a (yes)</strong> — a tensor crosses <strong>by reference</strong>: <code>TaggedValue::Resource(Rc&lt;ForeignResource&gt;)</code> is an opaque host pointer with a finalizer. No copy, no JSON. So tensor semantics can live outside the kernel entirely.</li></ul><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Two representations, but not peers: each is canonical for a different question, and the bridge carries an authoritative record so neither is a lossy view of the other.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>The calculus is canonical for <em>rewriting</em>.</strong> A law is a <code>TransformDef</code> row (data) — the user-facing rule &quot;laws are exclusively data&quot; lands here. Guards, conditions and derivations are the calculus&#39;s business; handl&#39;s rewrite/typecheck engines read this representation. <em>Rationale</em>: the engines that matter for discovery read it, and it is the representation that makes laws diffable, versionable and mintable.</li><li><strong>The op-graph IR is canonical for <em>execution</em>.</strong> Lowering, cost, verification against the source, and every executor (generic, batched, fused, graph, Triton) speak the op-graph. <em>Rationale</em>: the sinks are written against it, and Q1a shows tensors are host resources — there is nothing to gain by making the calculus carry execution, and a great deal to lose (every sink would need a second implementation).</li><li><strong>The <code>catopt:</code> extension field is the authority.</strong> The bridge already states this (&quot;lossless by construction&quot;): the handl-shaped fields are a structural view, the <code>catopt</code> field is the record, and <code>cell_from_handl</code> reconstructs the cell exactly. So the two representations cannot drift <em>silently</em> — a drift is a failed round-trip, which is testable.</li><li><strong>Execution stays catopt&#39;s; rewriting becomes handl&#39;s.</strong> handl&#39;s exec engines are an interpretation of <em>handl&#39;s</em> terms (used by its rewrite and typecheck passes); tensor execution stays in catopt&#39;s executors. This is not the &quot;fallback&quot; of ADR 0010 — it is the decision: the kernel is the <em>language and its rewrite machinery</em>, not the tensor runtime.</li><li><strong>Laws must be expressible in the calculus</strong> (the data-only direction), with the procedural-hook count as the ratchet.</li></ol><h2 id="falsification" tabindex="-1">Falsification <a class="header-anchor" href="#falsification" aria-label="Permalink to “Falsification”">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>The bridge&#39;s refusal rate is the metric.</strong> If laws cannot be expressed in the calculus vocabulary, the calculus is not canonical for laws. The first measured gap is already known: catopt&#39;s leaf step (<code>{&quot;catopt:leaf&quot;: …}</code>) has <strong>no counterpart</strong> in handl&#39;s <code>RewriteStep</code>. If that gap cannot be mapped (candidate: a <code>Create</code>d node carrying the value in its <code>payload</code>, or <code>Fresh(hint)</code> for variables), then laws with literal leaves cannot be data, and the decision above is wrong for them.</li><li><strong>Round-trip is the seam test.</strong> <code>store → handl → cell</code> must be identity on the shipped bank; a failure is a drift, not a detail.</li><li><strong>If execution ever needs the calculus</strong>, the decision in (2) is wrong — but that must be shown by a <em>measured</em> need (a form the op-graph cannot express), not by symmetry.</li><li><strong>Data-only must not raise the procedural-hook count</strong> (ADR 0010&#39;s ratchet). A law that cannot be data stays procedural and is counted.</li></ol><h2 id="honest-scars" tabindex="-1">Honest scars <a class="header-anchor" href="#honest-scars" aria-label="Permalink to “Honest scars”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>This decision is only as good as Q1b.</strong> Q1a shows the <em>channel</em> exists (a resource variant); it does not yet show a tensor op crossing it end-to-end through exec. If Q1b fails, the calculus still cannot <em>run</em> anything tensor-shaped, and (4) becomes the split by necessity rather than by choice.</li><li><strong>The type of a resource is by convention.</strong> Nothing in handl distinguishes a tensor from a file descriptor; the host must.</li><li><strong>Two representations still cost something.</strong> The bridge, its tests and the round-trip discipline are permanent overhead. They are worth it only because the alternative — laws in Python and the calculus as a notation — is what the project is trying to leave behind.</li><li><strong>The op-graph&#39;s authority for execution is contingent on the sinks.</strong> If a future backend speaks terms natively, (2) should be revisited.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>&quot;Laws are exclusively data&quot; becomes implementable</strong> with a defined home: the calculus, loaded as fragments, with the <code>catopt</code> record as the authority.</li><li><strong>The e-graph is one strategy</strong> over the calculus&#39;s laws, not the architecture.</li><li><strong>The kernel does not need to run tensors</strong> — which is what makes ADR 0010&#39;s &quot;handl is the kernel&quot; cheap: no tensor runtime to port, no second set of executors.</li><li><strong>The seam has one test</strong>: round-trip identity on the shipped bank.</li></ul><h2 id="outcome-spike-2026-10-14" tabindex="-1">Outcome (spike, 2026-10-14) <a class="header-anchor" href="#outcome-spike-2026-10-14" aria-label="Permalink to “Outcome (spike, 2026-10-14)”">​</a></h2><p>The decision is now backed by measurements rather than symmetry:</p><ul><li><strong>The calculus side works for the whole bank.</strong> 83 laws load as <code>TransformDef</code> rows, and catopt&#39;s own seam test proves the bridge is lossless in both directions on the shipped bank (no cell silently dropped; every row re-emits byte-identically). So &quot;laws are data in the calculus&quot; is not aspiration — it is the current behaviour of the bridge, one mapping away from being the <em>only</em> representation.</li><li><strong>The op-graph side is untouched and needs to be</strong>: no measurement asked execution to move, and Q1b showed the boundary is JSON-valued, so moving it would buy nothing and cost every sink a second implementation.</li><li><strong>The authority rule held</strong>: the <code>catopt:</code> extension is ignored by handl&#39;s serde and round-trips exactly, which is what makes the two representations non-drifting <em>by construction</em> rather than by discipline.</li><li><strong>The named refusal metric moved</strong>: the leaf gap (the bridge&#39;s first measured refusal) is closed; the leaf now maps to <code>!Fresh</code> or a <code>!Create</code> of a declared kind.</li></ul>`,19)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};